# Multi-Service Docker Compose Deployment Guide

A general, application-agnostic guide for containerizing a multi-service solution
(one or more backend services + one user-facing frontend/UI) with Docker Compose,
where **only the frontend is exposed to the outside world** and backend services
are reachable only from inside the Docker network.

This guide is based on a real deployment (3 services: two Python backends + one
Node/TypeScript frontend) but every step below is written generically so it can
be applied to any stack (Python, Node, Java, Go, etc.) and any number of services.

---

## 1. When to use this pattern

Use this approach whenever your solution has:
- One or more **backend services** (APIs, agents, workers) that only need to be
  called by *other services in the same solution*, never directly by end users.
- One **frontend/UI service** (or API gateway) that end users or other systems
  need to reach directly.

The goal: backend services stay private (no attack surface exposed to the
internet), only the frontend's port is published.

---

## 2. Prerequisites — check once per VM/host

```bash
docker --version
docker compose version
systemctl is-enabled docker   # should print "enabled" -> starts automatically on boot
systemctl is-active docker    # should print "active"
```

If `docker compose version` fails, you have the older standalone `docker-compose`
(with a hyphen) — the commands in this guide use the newer `docker compose`
(no hyphen, built into the Docker CLI); adjust if needed.

If your shell user gets `permission denied ... docker.sock`, either prefix every
command with `sudo`, or add yourself to the `docker` group once
(`sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`, then log out/in for it to take effect).

---

## 3. Step 1 — Inventory your services

Before writing any file, write down, for every service:

| Service name | Language/runtime | Port it listens on | Exposed to host? | Depends on |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `frontend` | e.g. Node | e.g. 4000 | **Yes** | backend-a, backend-b |
| `backend-a` | e.g. Python | e.g. 5000 | No | — |
| `backend-b` | e.g. Python | e.g. 5001 | No | — |

Only the row(s) marked "Yes" get a `ports:` entry in Compose later. Everything
else is reachable *only* from other containers on the same Compose network, by
service name.

---

## 4. Step 2 — One Dockerfile per service

Each service gets its **own** Dockerfile and builds its **own** image from its
**own** dependency manifest (`requirements.txt`, `package.json`, `go.mod`, etc.).

**Do not** try to merge multiple services' dependencies into one shared
image/virtualenv "to save time" — that's a local-dev convenience at best and
actively defeats the point of containers (isolation, independent versioning,
independent rebuilds). If you were tempted to do that for faster local
iteration outside Docker, don't carry that habit into the Dockerfiles.

### Python service template
```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim

# Any native/system libraries your Python deps need MUST be installed here,
# even if they're already installed on the host VM — containers have their
# own isolated filesystem and see none of the host's installed packages.
# Example (only if your app actually needs these, e.g. PDF rendering libs):
# RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \
#     libpango-1.0-0 libcairo2 \
#     && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY . .

EXPOSE <ACTUAL_PORT_THIS_SERVICE_BINDS_TO>

CMD ["python3", "your_entrypoint.py"]
```

### Node/TypeScript frontend template (multi-stage build)
```dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
RUN npm run build

FROM node:20-alpine AS production
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci --omit=dev
COPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist
ENV NODE_ENV=production
ENV PORT=<ACTUAL_PORT>
EXPOSE <ACTUAL_PORT>
CMD ["node", "dist/index.js"]
```

**Common pitfall — stale `EXPOSE`:** `EXPOSE` is documentation to Docker/Compose,
not enforcement, but it must match the port your app *actually* binds to in
code. Dockerfiles copy-pasted between environments/projects often end up with
an `EXPOSE` left over from an old port number. Before trusting any existing
Dockerfile, grep the actual source for where it binds its port
(`app.listen(...)`, `uvicorn.run(..., port=...)`, etc.) and make sure `EXPOSE`
agrees with it.

