## 5. Step 3 — Fix inter-service URLs (the #1 thing that breaks)

**`localhost` inside one container does not reach another container.** Each
container has its own private network namespace; `localhost` there means "this
container only." This is the single most common reason a working bare-metal
setup breaks the moment you containerize it.

**The fix:** Docker Compose gives every service a DNS name equal to its
service name in `docker-compose.yml`. From inside any container on the same
Compose network, `http://backend-a:5000/...` resolves correctly — no IP
addresses needed, no manual `/etc/hosts` editing.

**How to apply this without breaking your existing bare-metal/local-dev setup:**
Don't rewrite your committed `.env` files to use container service names —
that breaks running the same services directly on a VM/laptop outside Docker
later. Instead, override just the inter-service URL variables in
`docker-compose.yml` via `environment:`:

```yaml
services:
  frontend:
    environment:
      BACKEND_A_URL: "http://backend-a:5000"
      BACKEND_B_URL: "http://backend-b:5001"
```

This works because every mainstream `.env`-loading library (`dotenv` for
Node, `python-dotenv` for Python, etc.) **does not override a variable that
is already set in the process environment** — so a value Compose injects
before the process starts always wins over whatever's baked into a
committed `.env` file, with zero edits to that file.

