## 8. Step 6 — Build, run, verify

```bash
cd /path/to/project/root
sudo docker compose build              # build all images
sudo docker compose up -d              # start all containers, detached
sudo docker compose ps                 # check status + which ports are published
```

**Verification checklist — run every time you stand this up on a new host:**

```bash
# 1. The exposed service responds from the host:
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:<FRONTEND_PORT>/

# 2. Backend services do NOT respond on the host (confirms they're private):
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:<BACKEND_PORT>/
# expect a connection error/timeout, not an HTTP response

# 3. Internal Compose DNS actually works (run from inside the frontend container):
sudo docker compose exec frontend sh -c "curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://backend-a:<PORT>/"
```

**Cloud firewall / NSG reminder:** if this host is a cloud VM (Azure, AWS,
GCP, etc.) and you want the frontend reachable from the public internet (not
just the VM's private network), you must also open an inbound firewall rule
for the published port on the VM/instance's security group — Docker's own
port publishing is necessary but not sufficient on a cloud VM with a network
firewall in front of it.

**Don't test your own public IP from inside the same VM** to "confirm"
internet reachability — many cloud providers' NAT setups prevent a VM from
reaching its own public IP from itself ("hairpin NAT"), so a failed curl to
your own public IP from inside the VM is inconclusive. Always confirm
external reachability from a genuinely separate machine.

---

## 9. Day-to-day commands

```bash
sudo docker compose up -d                 # start (or restart stopped) all services
sudo docker compose up -d --build         # rebuild images after a code change, then start
sudo docker compose down                  # stop and remove containers (volumes/data untouched)
sudo docker compose logs -f <service>     # tail logs for one service
sudo docker compose ps                    # status + published ports of all services
sudo docker compose restart <service>     # restart just one service
```

---

## 10. Checklist for adapting this to a brand-new project

1. [ ] List every service, its port, and whether it needs to be reachable
       from outside the Docker network.
2. [ ] Write (or fix) one Dockerfile per service; confirm `EXPOSE` matches the
       port the app actually binds to in code.
3. [ ] Add any system/native library dependencies into each service's own
       Dockerfile (don't assume the host's installed libraries help you —
       they don't exist inside the container).
4. [ ] Find every place one service's config/`.env` references another
       service via `localhost` — these need a Compose-level `environment:`
       override pointing at the sibling's **service name**, not an edit to
       the committed `.env`.
5. [ ] Identify secrets/credential files and persistent state directories;
       mount them as `volumes:` instead of `COPY`-ing them into the image.
6. [ ] Write `docker-compose.yml`: one `ports:` entry total (on the exposed
       service), `restart: unless-stopped` everywhere, shared network.
7. [ ] `docker compose build && docker compose up -d`.
8. [ ] Run the 3-point verification checklist in Step 6 above.
9. [ ] If on a cloud VM, open the relevant inbound firewall/NSG rule for the
       exposed port, and confirm reachability from a separate machine.
10. [ ] Confirm `systemctl is-enabled docker` so the whole stack survives a
        VM reboot with zero manual intervention.
